From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Subject: Remailers

Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~ () writes:
> 
> Thanks for the info, Hal (I assume Hal, since it looks like he used the
> remailer in question to post this message). Question: how easy is it to
> trace a message sent through a remailer without encryption? I'm no mail
> expert. Isn't it all tracable, if nowhere else, then at least at the site
> which is doing the remailing?

  The general idea is to use multiple remailers with encryption and
delays/mixing. Only if the remailers collude can the message be traced to the
destination.
  
  Consider 3 Messages, M1,M2, and M3 being sent through three remailers to
three seperate destinations.

(M1,M2,M3) -> |Remailer1| -> (N1,N2,N3) -> |Remailer2| -> (O1,O2,O3)
   -> |Remailer3| -> (DEST1, DEST2, DEST3)

  The message groups, N & O are the messages M1,M2,M3 rearranged in
an order different from which they are sent, and encrypted with the
next remailer's key.

  If you are a spook attempting to trace the messages, each remailer
outputs 3 messages which look nothing like the input messages (because
they are reencrypted).  If the order of the output messages are
randomized and delayed randomly, it is not possible to tell whether
M1 equals N1, N2, or N3.

  Of course for maximum safety you will want to use a large chain
of remailers and queue lots of messages at each step instead of sending
them out immediately, or in small groups of three.


  One thing the cypherpunks need to do is build in some fault tolerant 
chaining, standardize the remailer interface better, and set up
a newsgroup for "anonymous return" Still, the current remailers are
adequate as long as you do everything carefully and make sure the
ones you are chaining are still running.


